<!-- FAILURE STRATEGY -->

<section class="container failure">

    <h2 class="section-title">
        If the Certification Does Not Lead to Better Interviews
    </h2>

    <p class="section-description">
        Do not automatically conclude that you need another certification.
        Diagnose the point where the career strategy is failing.
    </p>


    <div class="failure-box">

        <h3>
            A credential is only one part of the hiring equation.
        </h3>

        <p>
            If you finish a certification and your interview results do not
            improve, treat that outcome as data. The next step is diagnosis,
            not automatically buying another course.
        </p>


        <div class="failure-grid">


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>
                    No Employer Responses
                </h4>

                <p>
                    If applications are receiving almost no responses,
                    investigate resume quality, job targeting, keywords,
                    experience, portfolio evidence, and whether the jobs
                    actually value the certification.
                </p>

            </div>


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>
                    Interviews But No Offers
                </h4>

                <p>
                    If the certification helped you reach interviews but
                    you repeatedly fail to progress, focus on interview
                    preparation, communication, technical questions,
                    behavioral examples, and demonstrating your work.
                </p>

            </div>


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>
                    Employers Want Experience
                </h4>

                <p>
                    Build experience substitutes through projects,
                    volunteer work, freelance work, nonprofit projects,
                    apprenticeships, internships, contract work, or
                    realistic simulations.
                </p>

            </div>


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>
                    Certification Is Rarely Requested
                </h4>

                <p>
                    Stop making the certification the centerpiece.
                    Shift your attention toward the skills and tools
                    repeatedly requested by employers.
                </p>

            </div>


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>
                    Market Is Highly Competitive
                </h4>

                <p>
                    Consider an adjacent role where your existing experience
                    provides an advantage. Use that position as a bridge
                    toward your desired career.
                </p>

            </div>


            <div class="failure-card">

                <h4>
                    You Dislike the Actual Work
                </h4>

                <p>
                    That is useful information. A career that looks attractive
                    on paper may not fit the person doing the work every day.
                    Pivot before investing heavily.
                </p>

            </div>


        </div>

    </div>

</section>


<!-- CONTINUE PIVOT STOP -->

<section class="container decision">

    <h2 class="section-title">
        Continue, Pivot, or Stop?
    </h2>

    <p class="section-description">
        Use evidence rather than frustration or sunk-cost thinking
        to decide what happens next.
    </p>


    <div class="decision-grid">


        <div class="decision-card keep">

            <h3>CONTINUE</h3>

            <p>
                Continue when evidence shows that the strategy is improving.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Interview opportunities are increasing.</li>
                <li>Your skills are improving.</li>
                <li>Employers recognize your evidence.</li>
                <li>You can identify a clear next skill.</li>
                <li>The work remains reasonably interesting.</li>
            </ul>

        </div>


        <div class="decision-card pivot">

            <h3>PIVOT</h3>

            <p>
                Change the approach when the career may still be viable
                but your current entry strategy is weak.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Applications produce little response.</li>
                <li>Your target roles require more experience.</li>
                <li>An adjacent role is more accessible.</li>
                <li>You need stronger projects.</li>
                <li>You need a different specialization.</li>
            </ul>

        </div>


        <div class="decision-card stop">

            <h3>STOP</h3>

            <p>
                Stop investing heavily when evidence repeatedly indicates
                that the path is a poor fit.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>You dislike the actual work.</li>
                <li>The training is financially damaging.</li>
                <li>The credential is rarely relevant.</li>
                <li>Multiple strategy changes produce no improvement.</li>
                <li>Another path has stronger evidence.</li>
            </ul>

        </div>


    </div>

</section>


<!-- LOW MONEY STRATEGY -->

<section class="container checklist">

    <h2 class="section-title">
        If Money Is Tight, Use This Order
    </h2>

    <p class="section-description">
        The goal is to reduce financial risk while increasing employability.
    </p>


    <ul>

        <li>
            Research real jobs before buying training.
        </li>

        <li>
            Use free learning resources first.
        </li>

        <li>
            Sample the actual work before committing.
        </li>

        <li>
            Build at least one meaningful project.
        </li>

        <li>
            Start applying once you have enough evidence to demonstrate beginner ability.
        </li>

        <li>
            Track applications, employer responses, interviews, and offers.
        </li>

        <li>
            Fix the bottleneck before buying another credential.
        </li>

        <li>
            Choose certifications based on employer demand rather than advertising.
        </li>

        <li>
            Build experience through practical work wherever possible.
        </li>

        <li>
            Keep an adjacent career option available so one failed path
            does not force you to start completely over.
        </li>

    </ul>

</section>


<!-- FINAL CTA -->

<section class="cta">

    <h2>
        Build a Career Experiment, Not a Certification Collection
    </h2>

    <p>
        Your goal is not to accumulate the most certificates.
        Your goal is to become more employable. Learn the skill,
        produce evidence, test the market, measure the results,
        identify the bottleneck, and change your strategy when
        the evidence tells you to.
    </p>

</section>


